# ☕ Java — Constructors

> A **Constructor** is a special method that is automatically called when an object is created using `new`.

---

## 📌 Key Rules

- Same name as the class
- No return type (not even `void`)
- Automatically called when object is created
- Used to **initialize** the object
- Can accept parameters

---

## 📌 Types of Constructors

### 1. Default Constructor
> Provided by Java **automatically** if you write NO constructor at all.

```java
class Car {
    String brand;
    int speed;
    // Java adds this silently → Car() {}
}

Car c1 = new Car(); // works fine
```

---

### 2. No-arg Constructor
> You write it **manually** with no parameters.

```java
class Car {
    String brand;
    int speed;

    Car() {
        brand = "Unknown";
        speed = 0;
    }
}
```

---

### 3. Parameterized Constructor
> Accepts arguments to set values **at object creation** — cleanest way!

```java
class Car {
    String brand;
    int speed;

    Car(String brand, int speed) {
        this.brand = brand;   // this → refers to class variable
        this.speed = speed;
    }

    void drive() {
        System.out.println(brand + " is driving at " + speed + " km/h");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Car c1 = new Car("BMW", 200);
        Car c2 = new Car("Lambo", 250);

        c1.drive();  // BMW is driving at 200 km/h
        c2.drive();  // Lambo is driving at 250 km/h
    }
}
```

---

## 📌 this Keyword

> `this` refers to the **current object's variable** inside the constructor.
> Used when parameter name and class variable name are the **same**.

```java
Car(String brand, int speed) {
    this.brand = brand;  // this.brand = class variable
                         // brand      = parameter
    this.speed = speed;
}
```

---

## 📌 Constructor Overloading

> Multiple constructors in the same class with **different parameters**.
> Java picks the right one based on arguments passed.

```java
class Student {
    String name;
    int age;

    Student() {
        name = "Unknown";
        age = 0;
    }

    Student(String name) {
        this.name = name;
        age = 0;
    }

    Student(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s1 = new Student();               // Unknown, 0
        Student s2 = new Student("Raghav");       // Raghav, 0
        Student s3 = new Student("Raghav", 20);   // Raghav, 20
    }
}
```

---

## 📌 Calling Constructor from Constructor — `this()`

```java
class Student {
    String name;
    int age;

    Student() {
        this("Unknown", 0);  // calls parameterized constructor
    }

    Student(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }
}
```

---

## ⚠️ Important Rules to Remember

| Rule | Detail |
|---|---|
| Default constructor | Auto-provided **only if** you write NO constructor |
| Parameterized added | Java **removes** default constructor automatically |
| Fix | Add a no-arg constructor manually if needed |
| Not inherited | Constructors are **never** inherited by subclasses |
| `this()` | Must be the **first line** inside constructor |

---

## 🧠 Quick Recap

```
Constructor        →  Initializes object, auto-called on new
No return type     →  Not even void
Same name          →  Must match class name exactly
this.variable      →  Refers to class variable (not parameter)
Overloading        →  Multiple constructors, different parameters
this()             →  Call one constructor from another
Not inherited      →  Subclass does not get parent's constructor
```

